{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And Mizraim begot</b> — He mentions the descendants of Mizraim, but he does not mention their settlements as he did for the others, for in the children of Japheth he mentioned islands, and in the children of Cush he mentioned the land of Shinar and Asshur, and in the children of Canaan he mentioned the borders of their land, and likewise in the children of Shem. This was because the land of Mizraim's dwelling was well known, for it is called by his name, and all his sons dwelt around Egypt, and the name of their lands was also like their names. For thus we find for the Pathrusim — the land of Pathros, which is part of the land of Egypt, as it is written (Ezekiel 30:13–14): \"And I will put a terror in the land of Egypt, and I will desolate Pathros, upon the land of their inheritance.\" And likewise \"Lud and all the Arab\" — around Egypt, and their name and the name of their land are identical. And likewise the Philistim — their land is Pelesheth, and thus it is written (Exodus 15:14): \"The inhabitants of Pelesheth.\"\n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 10:13) said that these are names of provinces, and in each and every province there is one family, and therefore they are all in the plural. And the absolute proof is \"from whence they went out,\" for this is a reference to the place.\n\nAnd the meaning of \"which went out\" according to the commentators is that he begot them, in the language of \"And kings shall come out of your loins\" (Genesis 35:11).\n\nAnd Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 10:14) wrote: from both of them they went out — that the Pathrusim and the Casluchim would exchange wives with one another, and the Philistines went out from among them; Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 37:5).\n\nAnd in my opinion, according to the plain sense, the Casluchim were inhabitants of a city called by that name, and it was part of the land of Caphtor, where their kinsmen the Caphtorim dwelt, and they went out from there — from among the Caphtorim, who were of the seed of the Casluchim — and went to seek for themselves a resting place. And they left the land to their kinsmen, and conquered for themselves a land whose name is Pelesheth, and were afterward called Philistim after the name of the land. And this is what is written (Deuteronomy 2:22): \"The Caphtorim, who went out of Caphtor, destroyed them and dwelt in their stead\" — and they are of the children of the Casluchim, inhabitants of the land of Caphtor."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:27:11.752668267Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}